course: dictionary
lesson: 9585
title: One word, several meanings
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: The same word can have more than one meaning. A dictionary lists the meanings separately, and the context tells you which one to use. «The stream ran past our garden, cold and clear.» Here stream is a small, narrow river, because water runs through the garden.
explain: «A stream of cars left the car park after the match.» Here a stream is a continuous flow of things, and there is no water at all. Stream can also be a verb: «Tears streamed down her face» means the tears flowed down steadily.
explain: When you stream a film, you watch it straight from the internet, and it arrives in small pieces as you watch. Ask yourself: what is moving, and where is it coming from? The clues in the sentence will show you which meaning fits.
fact: Stream as a noun can mean a small, narrow river.
fact: Stream can also mean a continuous flow, like «a stream of people».
fact: Stream as a verb means to flow continuously, as in «tears streamed down her face».
fact: To stream a film or music means to play it straight from the internet, sent in small pieces as you watch or listen.
fact: Streams flow downhill and join to make rivers; they are homes for fish, insects and frogs. Never cross fast-moving stream water, because it can rise quickly after rain.
exercise: «The stream ran past our garden, cold and clear.» What is a stream here? | A group of pupils of similar level | *A small, narrow river | A film played from the internet | A flow of cars on a motorway
exercise: «A stream of cars left the car park after the match.» What does stream mean here? | To play a film online | A school group | *A continuous flow of things, like cars | A small river
exercise: «Tears streamed down her face.» What does streamed mean? | Dried in the sun | Pressed together | *Flowed continuously | Stopped suddenly
test: When you stream a film, where does it come from? | A library shelf | *The internet, sent in small pieces as you watch | Your school's photocopier | Only a cinema projector
test: In «Tears streamed down her face», is stream a noun or a verb? | Noun | Pronoun | *Verb | Adjective
exam: Which meaning fits «a stream of ducklings followed their mum»? | *A continuous flow of things, like a line of moving ducklings | A small river | To play a video online | A school group of similar level
